node_modules
.next
out
.env
.env.local
.env.production
# Anything alongside them. A hand made copy called .env.local.bak or .env.old
# holds every secret the real one does, and the exact names above would let it
# through.
.env.*
!.env.example
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*
.DS_Store
.next-verify/
# BUILD OUTPUT UNDER ANOTHER NAME.
#
# A build can be pointed at a different directory with PCD_DIST_DIR, which is
# how a verification build runs while a dev server still holds .next. Those
# directories are ordinary build output and must never be committed: each one
# carries a webpack cache of 300MB or so, well past what GitHub will accept,
# and a push carrying them is refused outright.
.nv-*/
.nv*/
# EVERY build directory, not a list of the ones we have thought of.
#
# The two rules above name .next-verify and .nv-anything, and a build written to
# .next-build matched neither. It went into a commit with 1277 files in it, and
# GitHub refused the push on the two webpack caches inside it:
#
#     .next-build/cache/webpack/server-production/0.pack is 246.15 MB
#     .next-build/cache/webpack/client-production/0.pack is 135.37 MB
#
# PCD_DIST_DIR can be given any name at all, so the pattern has to cover the
# shape rather than the names already used. Anything beginning .next that is a
# directory is build output.
.next*/
# And the logs a dev server is redirected into, which are not build output but
# are not ours to keep either.
.next-dev*.log

